# 2. THE NEW WAKE-WORD ACTIVATION SYSTEM

## Objective

The user should be able to activate LORIN by speaking naturally, rather than having to open the application and press a microphone button every time.

Support configurable wake phrases, including:

- "Hey LORIN."
- "LORIN, are you there?"
- "LORIN, let's get to work."
- "LORIN, can you check this?"
- "LORIN, I need your help."

The user should also be able to add, remove or change wake phrases.

## 2.1 Wake-word architecture

Implement a dedicated wake-word service with platform-specific adapters.

Preferred design:

1. Local wake-word detection.
2. Local voice activity detection.
3. A small on-device speech-processing pipeline.
4. Speech recognition after activation.
5. LORIN intent and command processing.
6. Backend AI inference when needed.

The wake-word detector should operate locally wherever technically possible.

Do not continuously stream raw microphone audio to Gemini, Ollama or another cloud service simply to detect the wake phrase.

The actual command may be transmitted to the selected AI provider only after the user has activated the assistant and the applicable privacy policy permits it.

Use a suitable, maintained wake-word engine with verified licensing and supported target platforms.

Do not implement a fake wake-word listener that activates merely because a button was clicked.

## 2.2 Supported listening states

Create an explicit listener state machine.

States:

- Listener disabled.
- Permission required.
- Permission denied.
- Preparing listener.
- Foreground listening.
- Background listening.
- Wake phrase detected.
- Assistant activating.
- Command recognition.
- Processing request.
- Speaking.
- Interrupted.
- Paused.
- Listener unavailable.
- Listener error.

Every state must have an accurate visual representation.

Provide an easily accessible control to:

- Enable listening.
- Disable listening.
- Pause listening.
- Resume listening.
- Select the microphone.
- Test microphone input.
- Configure wake phrases.
- View privacy information.
- View current listener capability.

A microphone indicator must remain visible whenever audio capture is active.

Do not hide microphone activity.

Do not secretly record, store or transmit conversations.

## 2.3 Browser and PWA listening behaviour

Implement actual foreground wake-word detection in the PWA only where the browser and operating system provide the required microphone capabilities.

Require:

- HTTPS or a secure context.
- Explicit microphone permission.
- User activation to start listening.
- A visible listening state.
- A clear stop control.
- Proper microphone release when the listener is disabled.

When the browser suspends the page, the microphone permission is revoked or the operating system stops audio capture, LORIN must report that listening has stopped.

Do not attempt to bypass browser lifecycle restrictions.

A service worker must not be presented as a permanently running microphone listener.

The PWA should continue to support:

- Tap-to-talk.
- Text conversation.
- Foreground voice interaction.
- Push notifications where supported and configured.
- Restoration of conversations and pending work.
- Automatic reconnection and synchronization.

**Do not advertise permanent background listening as a PWA capability unless it has been verified on that exact platform.**

## 2.4 Windows desktop background listener

Use the existing Tauri 2 direction to build the actual Windows desktop application.

The desktop application should support, subject to verified permissions and platform testing:

- Running in the system tray.
- Minimizing to tray.
- Optional launch at system startup.
- Optional background wake-word listening.
- Microphone selection.
- Visible listening status.
- A keyboard shortcut to activate LORIN.
- Notification-based activation.
- A setting to disable background listening.
- A setting to completely exit the application.

Autostart must be user-controlled and disabled until the owner explicitly enables it.

Closing the main window should offer the configured behaviour:

- Minimize to tray.
- Continue approved background services.
- Completely exit.

Never assume that closing the interface means the application has stopped.

Likewise, never claim that the background listener remains active after the native process has exited.

Build and test the actual Windows application before labelling this feature available.

## 2.5 Android and mobile background listening

Keep the PWA as the first mobile installation option.

For Android, implement background wake listening only through a genuine, permissioned native capability where supported.

When required, use an appropriately configured foreground service, persistent system notification, explicit microphone permission and compliant operating-system lifecycle handling.

The user must be able to see that the microphone listener is active and stop it immediately.

If Android battery management, system policy or application restrictions interrupt listening, show the actual interruption.

Do not silently restart a microphone service in violation of operating-system restrictions.

For iOS and unsupported mobile browsers, provide foreground listening and tap-to-talk. Do not promise continuous background microphone operation through a normal PWA.

A future native iOS implementation must be evaluated separately against supported platform capabilities and privacy rules.

## 2.6 Wake phrases must carry context

LORIN must distinguish between a simple wake phrase and a wake phrase that already contains a command.

Examples:

**User:** "Hey LORIN."

Expected behaviour: Activate and greet the user naturally.

**User:** "LORIN, are you there?"

Expected behaviour: Activate and respond that LORIN is available.

**User:** "LORIN, let's get to work."

Expected behaviour: Activate and open the appropriate working context.

**User:** "LORIN, can you check the Atlas project?"

Expected behaviour: Activate and retain the actual command. After the introduction, begin checking the Atlas project.

Do not discard the words spoken after the wake phrase.

Do not ask the user what to do when the user has already supplied the instruction.

